Ansible Error Handling

Today I learned about Error Handling in Ansible, which helps in controlling failures and managing task behavior intelligently ⚠️
In real automation, tasks may fail due to network issues, permissions, or configuration errors.
 Proper error handling ensures Playbooks continue safely and predictably 🔄

🔹 🚫 ignore_errors
The ignore_errors: yes keyword allows a Playbook to continue even if a task fails.
Example use case:
 • Non-critical task failure
 • Optional package installation
 • Temporary service issues
This prevents the entire Playbook from stopping due to a minor error.

🔹 ❌ failed_when
failed_when is used to define custom failure conditions.
Instead of default failure behavior, we can control when a task should be marked as failed.
 This is useful when command output determines success or failure 🎯
It gives more control over automation logic.

🔹 🔄 changed_when
changed_when controls whether a task is marked as “changed” or not.
This helps in:
 • Accurate reporting
 • Avoiding unnecessary handler triggers
 • Better automation visibility
It improves efficiency and prevents false change results ⚡

🔹 🐞 Debug Module
The debug module is used for troubleshooting and displaying variable values.
Common uses:
 • Print variable values
 • Check outputs
 • Troubleshoot Playbooks
It is one of the most useful modules during development and testing 🧪

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
Error handling makes automation resilient.
 Custom conditions improve control over tasks.
 Debugging helps identify issues faster.
